INT. AIRPORT HOTEL BAR - DELAYED FLIGHTS - NIGHT

A beige hotel bar with a TV muted to a weather map. Red banquettes. A long counter with five stools. A bowl of stale pretzels. A neon sign: FLIGHTS CANCELLED.

At the bar, ROSALIND ABERNATHY-OKORO (50s, travel-wrinkled blazer, a tote bag of paperbacks) nurses a vodka tonic. Two stools down, WALTER "WALT" ZIELINSKI-ADEYEMI (60s, rumpled, a cowboy hat that doesn't belong in Ohio) sips a beer. In the banquette, a young couple, BRIDGET OSEI-LINDQVIST (20s, nose ring, laptop) and her boyfriend, KOFI ASANTE-MARCHETTI (20s, athletic, a guitar case). The bartender, DELPHINE "DEL" NWOKORO-HAYES (40s, sleeves rolled, tattoos), polishes a glass.

DEL
Last call's in ten.

ROSALIND
It's seven-thirty.

DEL
Airport rules. The hotel's full. I'm trying to get everyone fed.

WALT
I'll take anything with cheese.

DEL
That's the pretzels.

WALT
Then I'll take the pretzels.

He grabs the bowl. Rosalind reaches for it at the same time. They both stop, hands on opposite rims.

ROSALIND
Please.

WALT
Ladies first.

ROSALIND
That's very kind.

She takes a pretzel. Chews. Grimaces. Hands the bowl back. Walt laughs.

BRIDGET
(from the booth)
Kofi, they just cancelled again.

KOFI
Again?

BRIDGET
Seventh time.

KOFI
(to the bar)
Do they ever fly?

DEL
Sometimes.

KOFI
Which times?

DEL
When I'm not working.

Rosalind chuckles. Bridget closes her laptop and slides out of the booth, folding her arms.

BRIDGET
I'm going to scream.

KOFI
Don't.

BRIDGET
I'm going to scream, Kofi.

KOFI ^
You are not going to scream.

DEL
Please don't.

BRIDGET
Why not?

DEL
Because I'm the only person who knows where the good whiskey is.

Bridget laughs despite herself. Kofi, relieved, puts his arm around her.

WALT
(raising his beer)
To the weather.

ROSALIND
To the weather.

DEL
To last call.

They clink. Outside, thunder rolls. The lights dip. The TV flickers to a blue screen. Everyone looks up.

KOFI
That's a bad sign.

BRIDGET
That's the weather.

DEL
That's the cable.

WALT
It's all the same thing.

Rosalind finishes her drink and sets it down, gently.

ROSALIND
Does anyone play cards?

WALT
I do.

BRIDGET
We do.

KOFI
Not well.

ROSALIND
Better.

DEL
There's a deck behind the counter.

She fishes it out. Slides it down the bar. Walt catches it, shuffles with a flourish, and cards fly everywhere. They all burst out laughing. It is the first time anyone has laughed all night.

WALT
Poker?

ROSALIND
Rummy.

BRIDGET
Go Fish.

KOFI
Anything.

DEL
Fine. But I'm dealing.

She comes around the counter, and for the first time all night, everyone sits at the same table.
